        "comments": [
          {
            "count": 0,
            "is_markdown": false,
            "text": "Since R 4.4.0, a byte-compiled read of an active binding calls the\nbinding's function twice, where interpreted evaluation calls it once. If\nthe function replaces its own binding, the compiled read returns the new\nbinding's value instead of the function's result.\n\nReproducible example: attached as bceval-single-lookup-repro.R; the\ncompiled reads return 2 and 99, expected 1 and 42.\n\nAnalysis:\n\nSince r86189, the fallback in DO_GETVAR (src/main/eval.c) calls\nfindVarEX() to look for a promise it can force inline, then calls\ngetvar(), which repeats the lookup.\n\nProposed patch: attached as bceval-single-lookup.diff, against r90881.\ngetvar_force(), split out of getvar(), handles the value already found.\nThe fallback also continues from ENCLOS(rho) when the local frame has no\ncell to cache, instead of searching that frame twice more, as\nFIND_VAR_NO_CACHE() did before r77221. That halves the cost of reading a\nvariable from an enclosing environment: on arm64 macOS, closures making\nsuch reads ran 10 to 30% faster. Regression tests are in reg-tests-1e.R;\nmake check passes.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/480,\nwhere CI results and more benchmark details can be seen.",
